        "status": "success",
        "rationale": "**Obligation.** The requirement has one behavioral clause. When implementation shows end-user-observable behavior that no requirement covers, the `habeas-requirements` skill MUST direct agents to do three things. They draft a requirement for it, take it through review, agreement and commit, and do all of that before implementing the behavior. The skill's text is `assets/skills/habeas-requirements/SKILL.md`. The tests check that text.\n\n**What the skill says.** The Workflow section says: \"Behavior no requirement covers goes back to step 2.\" Step 2 is \"Draft.\" Step 3 is \"Review.\" Step 4 is \"Present each group to the user…\" and ends with the **Gate:** \"no implementation or test code, and no commit, until the user has agreed to, rejected, or deferred every group\". Step 5 is \"Commit… then commit it, all groups together, before any code\". Step 6 is \"Implement and test\".\n\n**Test mapping.**\n- `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_requirements_skill_drafts_a_requirement_for_uncovered_behavior]` covers three parts:\n  - The routing sentence. It uses `assert_phrases` to assert that the skill contains \"Behavior no requirement covers goes back to step 2\".\n  - The draft step. It asserts step 2 starts with \"Draft.\".\n  - The review and agreement steps. It asserts step 3 starts with \"Review.\" and step 4 starts with \"Present each group to the user\".\n- `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_requirements_skill_commits_every_group_together_before_code]` covers the commit and \"before implementing\" parts. It asserts that the commit step reads \"then commit it, all groups together, before any code\", and that it comes after the step containing **Gate:** (the agreement gate).\n\nTogether these pin the whole path the requirement names: routing to the draft step, the review, agreement and commit steps, and the order that puts commit before code. The assertions would fail if the routing sentence were removed or the steps were renumbered, reordered or renamed. Both tests carry `{test}` references to the subject.\n\n**Gaps.** I found no unaddressed obligation. The tests are textual checks of the skill, which is how this project verifies skill-content requirements. The requirement does not constrain wording beyond directing agents through these steps."
